ight mouth, $l > 0$"],["line","r","$l$ constant, at $1$, $2$, $3$ and $4\\,r_0$"],["line","r2","the same distances on the side of the left mouth, $l < 0$"],["line","throat","the throat $l = 0$, the smallest circle, of radius $r_0$"],["line","meridian","$\\phi$ constant, every $15°$"]],"turn":{"origins":[[0.0,0.0]],"meridians":24,"tint":{"sheet":"cover"},"marks":[]}},"settings":"$r_0 = 1$, the unit of every length.","input":"$\\Phi = 0$ and $r = \\sqrt{r_0^2 + l^2}$, the Ellis-Bronnikov wormhole; on $\\theta = \\pi/2$ neither $g$ nor $F$ enters the surface.","caption":["The plane $\\theta = \\pi/2$ through the wormhole at one moment of $t$, across the axis of the acceleration, drawn as a surface in flat space with every distance along it the metric distance. On it the metric is $dl^2 + r^2d\\phi^2$, so $dz/dl = \\sqrt{1 - (\\partial_l r)^2}$, and with $r = \\sqrt{r_0^2 + l^2}$ the surface is the catenoid $r = r_0\\cosh(z/r_0)$.","Neither $g$ nor $F$ enters this plane, where $\\cos\\theta = 0$, and the surface is the same at every moment of the trip. Both sides flatten into one and the same space outside, the left mouth and the right mouth a distance apart in it that changes as the right one travels, while the length through the throat stays as it is."]}]}
